[[SECTION]]
UID: SEC-SAFETY-3
TITLE: 3. Residual risk

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What remains after L1 to L6, stated without softening. Each entry names who
carries it, and relates to the safety goal whose layers leave it, or to the
hazard directly where there is no goal.
<<<

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**Welded contactor with a shorted SSR.** Both series devices conducting leaves the controller with no means of interrupting the heater circuit.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
There is no third interrupting device inside the boundary.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
**The L5 cutout and the operator.**
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SR-27] discriminates the case within about 4 s and latches fault 22, whose operator instruction is to isolate at the supply; the alarm sounds; [LINK: SR-30] warns of the intermittent mismatches that precede outright relay failure.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-03
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-02
STATEMENT: >>>
**A three-phase kiln cannot be fired safely with this controller.** It measures one phase, so a fault on either of the other two would be caught only by the thermal backstop, slowly, and the power and energy figures would cover a third of the load.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
Three-phase support was dropped on 2026-10-06 rather than half-built, because partial electrical cover is the kind that gets trusted.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installer, who must not fit this controller to a three-phase kiln.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Stated as out of scope in the README, in [requirements §12](requirements.sdoc) and in [LINK: ASM-10], rather than left as a limitation to be discovered. The thermal rules still protect a single-phase kiln in full.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-05
  ROLE: Remains under
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-09
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**A CT fitted to the wrong conductor, or clipped around two conductors** (net current about 0) makes the entire electrical channel blind, or makes it trip on every run.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
The controller cannot tell a correctly fitted CT reading zero from a wrongly fitted one.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installer.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Commissioning verifies a plausible reference current before the first firing; [LINK: FR-CUR-11] distinguishes "no signal at all" from "zero current"; a disabled or failed channel raises warning 111 continuously rather than going quiet.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-05
  ROLE: Remains under
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-09
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**A systematic error in the safety rules themselves**: a wrong threshold, an inverted comparison, a timer that never advances, is not caught by L2 or L3, since both would be working as written.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
L3 checks liveness, not correctness.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project, through verification.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
100 % of safety decision branches covered by automated host tests ([LINK: TR-19]); every `SR` covered by at least one automated test, inspection alone insufficient ([LINK: TR-23]); rules are pure functions driven directly by tests ([LINK: TR-09]); CI blocks merge on any failure ([LINK: TR-24]). **L5 remains the only protection genuinely independent of this risk.**
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-01
  ROLE: Remains under
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-04
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Common-cause failure of the MCU and its rails** defeats L2, L3 and L4 together.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
One MCU, one supply.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The design, by choice.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Every one of those layers fails *towards* de-energised, so the common-cause outcome is the safe state rather than an unsafe one. L5 is unaffected.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-01
  ROLE: Remains under
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-02
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**An operator who widens thresholds to their limits** reduces the effectiveness of detection without disabling it.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
[LINK: SR-22] bounds the ranges but permits the full range.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The operator.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Ranges are bounded; [LINK: SR-23]'s 1350 °C ceiling is a compile-time constant no configuration can raise; nothing can be disabled outright; untuned gains and disabled monitoring are annunciated as standing warnings (108, 111).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-06
  ROLE: Remains under
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-10
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**Network-borne program edits.** [LINK: FR-WEB-26] removed every route that can heat the kiln, so a firing can no longer be started, altered or aborted over the network. What remains is that an attacker can edit a stored firing *program*, which takes effect only if an operator then starts it at the kiln.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
Program authoring on a keyboard is the one thing a rotary encoder and a 128x64 OLED are genuinely bad at, so it was kept deliberately.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner of the network ([LINK: ASM-05]), and the operator who confirms the start.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Authentication with constant-time comparison and rate-limited failures ([LINK: NFR-19]); every request body and parameter treated as untrusted; no cloud connectivity and no remote access outside the local network by design. The device is **not** to be exposed to the internet.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-12
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**Hot surfaces and residual heat** ([LINK: HZ-11]) and **mains exposure during installation or service** ([LINK: HZ-07]).
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
Inherent to the equipment.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installation and the operator.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: HR-18] on CT isolation, creepage and clearance; [LINK: HR-16] forbidding a CT whose burden can be disconnected; documentation obligations ([LINK: NFR-26], [LINK: SR-24]).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-07
  ROLE: Remains against
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-11
  ROLE: Remains against

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-10
STATEMENT: >>>
**A door interlock is optional** ([LINK: HR-21] is a *should*, not a *shall*). The board now carries the series contact and the sense divider (2026-10-06), so fitting one is wiring rather than redesign, but a kiln without a switch still has nothing against [LINK: HZ-13], and the controller cannot tell the difference between "not fitted" and "fitted and shut" except by being told.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
Many existing kilns have no interlock, and retro-fitting one means working on the door furniture.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installer.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Warning 113 is raised continuously whenever no interlock is configured, so the gap is visible on the display and in the API rather than silent. [LINK: SR-31]'s normally-closed requirement means a *fitted* switch cannot fail silently.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-13
  ROLE: Remains under

[RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: RR-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**Unattended firing**, contrary to [LINK: ASM-06], removes L6 entirely.
<<<
WHY_IT_REMAINS: >>>
The firmware cannot verify attendance.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The operator.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Stated in the README, in [LINK: SR-24], and here. Unattended firing is **not** a design goal and the layered concept does not assume it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SG-07
  ROLE: Remains under

